  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1215 AM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nSouthwesterly flow will persist over the region through Friday as \nthe upper-level trough remains along the west coast. This setup will \nbring temperatures down slightly and allow for daily chances for \nafternoon showers and thunderstorms, mainly in Southwest Montana and \nalong the Continental Divide. Breezy southwest winds will also be \npresent during this time across the region, although it will be a \nfar cry from what was observed on Wednesday.\n\nThrough the weekend, the closed low over the PacNW shifts slightly \nbut maintains south/southwesterly flow over the region which will \ndraw up more tropical moisture into Montana. On Monday, the low \nejects a shortwave trough over the region which has the chance to \nbring more widespread wetting rain through Tuesday.\n\n\n- Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nWinds on Thursday and Friday:\n\nBreezy southwesterly winds will be possible across Southwest Montana \nthis afternoon with afternoon relative humidity in the 20s expected. \nThis will keep fire weather concerns elevated for portions of \nSouthwest Montana where a Red Flag Warning remains in effect through \nthe evening. While stronger winds are possible along north-south \noriented valleys including Ennis, there is low confidence in \nreaching high wind criteria with gusts expected to max out at around \n45 mph. Across the rest of the plains and Central Montana, gusts \nbetween 25 and 35 mph are likely but much stronger gusts are not \nexpected. A similar setup is expected on Friday with scattered \nthunderstorms possible which may bring locally stronger wind gusts.\n\n\nRain Sunday through Tuesday:\n\nBased on the latest model guidance, precipitation starts to fill in \nacross the region Sunday morning into the afternoon with the \nheaviest moisture expected on Monday as the shortwave passes \nthrough. Lingering showers will continue through Tuesday morning and \ntaper off from south to north through the afternoon. There is still \nuncertainty regarding precipitation amounts but right now the Hi-\nLine is the most favored for higher amounts while Southwest Montana \nis more favored for the low-end amounts.\n\nOver the 48 hour period covering Sunday morning through Tuesday \nmorning...\n\n - For amounts greater than 0.1\" there is a 80% probability of \noccurrence across the region.\n\n - For amounts greater than 0.5\" there is a 70% probability of \noccurrence north of a line between Helena and White Sulphur Springs \nand a 50% chance south of that line.\n\n - For amounts greater than 1.0\" there is a 50% probability of \noccurrence north of a line between Helena and White Sulphur Springs \nand a 25% chance south of that line.\n\n - For amounts greater than 1.5\" there is a 40% probability of \noccurrence north of a line between Great Falls and Lewistown and a \nless than 15% chance south of that line.\n\nExact amounts are likely to change but there is increasing \nconfidence that this shortwave will at least bring minor relief to \nsome of the recent fire weather concerns. -thor",
